   "id": "e2-91",
   "name": "Basic sentence structure: subject, verb, object"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "An English sentence usually follows the order Subject – Verb – Object (SVO).\n• The subject is who or what does the action: “The choir”.\n• The verb is the action or state: “sang”.\n• The object receives the action: “a traditional song”.\n→ “The choir (S) sang (V) a traditional song (O).”\nSome sentences have no object: “The baby sleeps.” (S V). Others add a complement or an adverbial: “My uncle is a musician.” (S V C); “We danced in the hall.” (S V A).\nIn questions the order changes: “Did the choir sing?” Adjectives come before the noun: “a beautiful song”, not “a song beautiful”.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Label the parts of “Ngum plays the balafon.”",
  "steps": [
   "Who does the action? Ngum — the subject.",
   "The action is “plays” — the verb; what is played? “the balafon” — the object."
  ],
  "answer": "Ngum (S) plays (V) the balafon (O).",
  "checks": []
 },
